PERATIONS=0ABRIEF=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AISSUE #19 =C2=A0=C2=B7=C2=A0 JULY 30, 20=
26=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AMost critical vendor lists are still organi=
zed around what enters the building.=0AThe operation now depends just as he=
avily on what enters the workflow: ERP=0Apartners, cloud platforms, AI prov=
iders, the one certified technician within=0Adriving distance. Any of them =
can stop order flow. Most of them are not on the=0Asame list.=0A=0AThis iss=
ue is about the register that puts them there, and the questions it=0Aforce=
s you to answer.=0A=0ABill=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0ATHE SHIFT=0A=0A=0AThe Critical =
Vendor List Is Too Short.=0A=0AThe list tracks what enters the building. Th=
e dependency tracks what enters the=0Aworkflow.=0A=0ATennant Company made t=
he distinction impossible to ignore. Its North America ERP=0Acutover in Nov=
ember 2025 introduced what CEO David Huml called =E2=80=9Csevere system=0Af=
unctionality issues that limited our ability to enter orders, ship products=
,=0Aand service our customers.=E2=80=9D=0A[https://seekingalpha.com/article=
/4874083-tennant-company-tnc-q4-2025-earnings-call-transcript]=0AOrder mana=
gement, production scheduling, fulfillment, and inventory visibility=0Aall =
suffered. Tennant put the damage at roughly $30 million in Q4 net sales and=
=0Aabout $22 million in adjusted EBITDA (SEC Form 8-K, Feb 2026=0A[https://=
www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/97134/000009713426000004/0000097134-26-0000=
04-index.htm]).=0AThat is not an IT inconvenience. It is a production and r=
evenue stoppage caused=0Aby a digital dependency.=0A=0AThree signals from 2=
026=0A=0A=C2=B7 65% of large companies now rank third-party and supply-chai=
n vulnerabilities=0Aas their greatest cyber-resilience challenge (up from 5=
4% in 2025). Only 33%=0Acomprehensively map their supply-chain ecosystems (=
WEF Global Cybersecurity=0AOutlook 2026=0A[https://www.weforum.org/publicat=
ions/global-cybersecurity-outlook-2026/digest/]).=0A=0A=C2=B7 83% of execut=
ives expect partner networks to expand over the next one to three=0Ayears. =
Only 18% have third-party risk management fully integrated with=0Aenterpris=
e risk management (KPMG Global TPRM Survey, March 2026=0A[https://kpmg.com/=
us/en/articles/2026/global-third-party-risk-management-survey.html]).=0A=0A=
=C2=B7 16% of organizations don=E2=80=99t track the creation of new AI-rela=
ted identities.=0AFewer than 25% have formal creation or removal policies. =
Service accounts, API=0Akeys, tokens, certificates, and AI-agent credential=
s now outnumber human=0Aidentities (Cloud Security Alliance, 2026=0A[https:=
//cloudsecurityalliance.org/artifacts/state-of-nhi-and-ai-security-survey-r=
eport]).=0A=0AThe 65% figure is a large-company number, not an SMB benchmar=
k. The lesson=0Atravels down-market anyway. Concentration is dangerous when=
 dependencies are not=0Avisible, and the AI identity layer is expanding fas=
ter than most procurement=0Afunctions can see.=0A=0AThe shift is not from s=
upplier risk to technology risk. It is from=0Acategory-based reviews to ope=
rational-dependency reviews. The operators pulling=0Aahead ask the same fou=
r questions of every dependency: what stops, how long does=0Arecovery take,=
 who owns the response, and what bypass has been tested.=0A=0ASteel, ERP, c=
loud hosting, an AI agent, and the only certified technician in the=0Aregio=
n belong on one list if any can halt the same order.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AFROM =
THE FLOOR=0A=0A=0AManage lock-in through architecture.=0A=0ASteve Bronson, =
CIO of Southern Glazer=E2=80=99s Wine & Spirits, on keeping options open=0A=
=0ASteve Bronson is CIO at Southern Glazer=E2=80=99s Wine & Spirits, the la=
rgest US wine=0Aand spirits distributor. Speaking with TechTarget in March,=
 he drew the=0Adistinction between operators who lose control of their depe=
ndencies and those=0Awho preserve them.=0A=0A=E2=80=9CVendor lock-in is a r=
eal risk, and it=E2=80=99s not going away. The key is managing it=0Athrough=
 architecture.=E2=80=9D=0A=0ASteve Bronson=0A=0ACIO, Southern Glazer=E2=80=
=99s Wine & Spirits =C2=A0=C2=B7=C2=A0 TechTarget, March 2026=0A[https://ww=
w.techtarget.com/searchcio/feature/CIO-risk-management-Lessons-from-Souther=
n-Glazers-CIO]=0A=0ABronson=E2=80=99s approach: define core ecosystems acro=
ss HR, supply chain, and ERP, and=0Agive the third party design authority i=
nside that core. Around it, use service=0Alayers, redundancy, and bypass pa=
ths to keep the key decisions reversible.=0A=0AThe takeaway=0A=0AThe distin=
ction is sharper than =E2=80=9Cavoid lock-in.=E2=80=9D Define the core, ass=
ign=0Aauthority, preserve a bypass, and keep every high-impact decision rev=
ersible.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0ATHE STACK=0A=0A=0AThe Critical Dependency Registe=
r.=0A=0AOne list for every outside dependency that can stop the operation.=
=0A=0ASame fields, same review, same accountability, whether the dependency=
 is a=0Asupplier, a system, or a single certified person.=0A=0ABuild one li=
ving register for every outside dependency that can interrupt an=0Aoperatin=
g workflow. Physical suppliers, ERP and software platforms, cloud and=0ASaa=
S, AI providers and agents, contractors, credentialed specialists. All in o=
ne=0Aplace, all scored the same way.=0A=0AWhat each row records=0A=0A=C2=B7=
 The dependency and the workflows or sites it affects.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Operatio=
nal impact and recovery time. Scored against outcomes: degraded work,=0Adel=
ayed shipment, stopped line, stopped order flow, customer-service failure.=
=0A=0A=C2=B7 Business owner and technical owner. Two names, not a departmen=
t.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Fallback plan and last fallback test date. Untested does not=
 count as tested.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Contract, credential, license, or certificati=
on expiration. The dates that=0Aquietly turn a working dependency into a br=
oken one.=0A=0AWhat the operator gets out of it=0A=0AFour outputs, every cy=
cle=0A=0A1. Heatmap. High-impact dependencies with no tested fallback, rank=
ed.=0A=0A2. Change log. New integrations, providers, credentials, contracto=
rs, expiring=0Acertifications.=0A=0A3. Owner queue. Overdue fallback tests =
and unresolved single points of failure,=0Aby name.=0A=0A4. Drill calendar.=
 A short list of the few dependencies whose failure would stop=0Aorders, pr=
oduction, or shipping.=0A=0ACadence: weekly exception check, monthly owner =
attestation, quarterly fallback=0Adrill for the highest-impact rows. Refres=
h the register whenever a supplier,=0Aintegration, owner, contract, credent=
ial, AI agent, or certified role changes.=0A=0ATwo paths to build it=0A=0AP=
ath 1: Internal build=0A=0AStart with one value stream, order entry through=
 shipment. Put operations,=0Aprocurement, IT, finance, HR, quality, and ser=
vice in one working session. List=0Aevery external dependency at each hando=
ff and score it against the shared=0Afields. Do not mark a fallback complet=
e without an owner and a test date. A=0Aspreadsheet or lightweight database=
 is enough for the first cycle.=0A=0APath 2: ERP or partner build=0A=0AUse =
the existing ERP partner or supply-chain consultant to map integrations,=0A=
scheduled jobs, connectors, custom workflows, privileged roles, support pat=
hs,=0Aand manual bypasses. Give that partner design authority inside the de=
fined core=0A(Bronson=E2=80=99s point). The operator retains ownership of i=
mpact scores, recovery=0Atargets, and risk acceptance. Strong partners add =
change alerts and fallback=0Atesting after go-live.=0A=0APut every dependen=
cy that can stop order flow on one list before the next outage=0Awrites it =
for you.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0ATHE OPERATIONS BRIEF=0A=0ABy Bill Murphy =C2=A0=
=C2=B7=C2=A0 Powered by Colony Spark [https://www.colonyspark.com/]=0A=0AAl=
ready have a critical dependency register=3F Or watching a client find out =
the=0Ahard way what should have been on it=3F Hit reply, I read every one. =
Bill=0A=0AColony Spark =C2=B7 170 Mason Street =C2=B7 Greenwich, Connecticu=
t 06830, United States=0A=0AYou=E2=80=99re receiving this because you subsc=
ribed to The Operations Brief.=0AUnsubscribe [[[custom:unsubscribe]]]
